  <title>Print a label</title>

  <p>LinuxZPL sends labels straight to a Zebra printer, on the network or
  plugged into this computer by USB, with no printing system or printer
  driver in between. If the printer cannot be reached, the error says why.</p>

  <section id="default-printer">
    <title>The printer you normally use</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Settings</gui><gui>Default Printer…</gui></guiseq> holds
    the printer and its <gui>DPI</gui>, its resolution. Under
    <gui>Connection</gui>, choose <gui>Network</gui> and give the printer's
    <gui>Address</gui> and <gui>Port</gui> (usually 9100), or <gui>USB</gui>
    and choose it from the <gui>Printer</gui> list: see
    <link xref="#usb"/>. <gui>Test Connection</gui> checks the printer can be
    reached and asks it its resolution. <gui>Font
    memory</gui> says where on the printer fonts are kept: see
    <link xref="fonts"/>. These are remembered from one session to the
    next.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="print-window">
    <title>The Print window</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Print…</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>P</key></keyseq>) opens the Print window.
    <gui>Print</gui> prints, <gui>OK</gui> keeps the settings without
    printing, and <gui>Cancel</gui> changes nothing.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Printer</gui></title>
        <p>The printer to print to, on the network or by USB, as in
        <gui>Default Printer</gui>. A change here lasts until LinuxZPL is
        closed and is not remembered; <gui>Use Default</gui> fills in the
        default printer again. This is how to print to another printer for
        a while.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Copies (^PQ)</gui></title>
        <p>How many labels to print, and optionally a pause after every so
        many (<gui>Pause every</gui>, 0 for never). The printer prints the
        copies itself. <gui>Replicates</gui> is how many replicates of each
        serial number to print before it changes; 0, the default, changes it
        on every label.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Label</gui></title>
        <p><gui>Print upside down</gui>, <gui>Mirror left to right</gui>,
        <gui>Reverse fields, white on black</gui>, and <gui>Check barcode
        data as printed</gui>, which has the printer check each barcode's data
        and print <sys>INVALID</sys> and an error code in place of one that is
        not valid.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Printer setup sent with the label</gui></title>
        <p><gui>Darkness</gui>, the print, slew and backfeed speeds, the
        print mode (such as tear off, peel off or cutter), the media
        tracking and the media type. Each starts at <gui>Printer's
        setting</gui>, which sends nothing and leaves the printer as it was
        set up. A value you choose stays in effect on the printer after the
        label has printed.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>Everything but the printer is saved with the label, so the label
    prints the same way next time, and a change to it can be undone.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="usb">
    <title>A printer plugged in by USB</title>
    <p>Choose <gui>USB</gui> under <gui>Connection</gui>, then the printer in
    the <gui>Printer</gui> list, which names each one with its device, such
    as <sys>lp2</sys>. Press <gui>Refresh</gui> after plugging a printer in
    or switching it on. LinuxZPL remembers the printer by its serial number,
    so it finds it again when it comes back as another device after a
    restart. A remembered printer that is not plugged in stays in the list as
    <gui>(not connected)</gui>.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>No permission</title>
        <p>The printer's device belongs to the <sys>lp</sys> group. Join it
        with <cmd>sudo usermod -aG lp $USER</cmd>, then log out and in
        again.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>(no usblp driver)</title>
        <p>The printer is plugged in, but has no device, because a print
        system's own driver took it over to look at it and did not give it
        back. This can happen when a new printer is plugged in. The error
        gives the command that gives it back, which needs your password:
        <cmd>echo 3-1.1.2.1:1.0 | sudo tee
        /sys/bus/usb/drivers/usblp/bind</cmd>, with the printer's own
        number in place of <sys>3-1.1.2.1:1.0</sys>. Unplugging it and
        plugging it in again may not help, since the same thing happens
        again.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>A "missing printer driver" message</title>
        <p>When a new printer is plugged in, the desktop may say it has no
        driver for it. LinuxZPL does not need one, so the message can be
        ignored. Setting up a print queue for the printer is not needed
        either, and while a print queue is printing, LinuxZPL cannot use the
        printer.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>In use</title>
        <p>Only one program can use a USB printer at a time. LinuxZPL waits a
        few seconds for whatever has it, and then says it is in use.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>

  <section id="quick-print">
    <title>Quick Print</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Quick Print</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>P</key></keyseq>) prints at
    once, to the printer in use and with the label's settings as they
    are.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="before">
    <title>Before it prints</title>
    <list>
      <item><p>If the label uses fonts of its own, LinuxZPL asks the printer
      whether it has them, and offers to send any it lacks: see
      <link xref="fonts"/>.</p></item>
      <item><p>Elements whose <gui>Print This Element</gui> is cleared are
      not printed.</p></item>
      <item><p>The label always tells the printer whether to print upside
      down, mirrored or reversed, and which character set it is in, since a
      printer otherwise keeps whatever the last job set.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>
</page>
